From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: B Honey <9922368+Mhsbrian@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] OPENBOOK: guest map shadow: clear executable bits a word at a
 time

Every guest mmap, mprotect and munmap that isn't executable calls
GuestMapShadow::ClearExecutable over its whole range, and it cleared the
shadow bitmap one page at a time, writing every byte. A JIT engine maps,
protects and unmaps huge never-executable ranges (V8 reserves gigabytes),
so each call cost one step per 4 KiB of the range and dirtied the shadow
with zeros, with nothing to invalidate. On the G14 that was about 10% of
a browser engine's CPU (ContentShell, ANAL-379) with almost no
invalidations.

ClearExecPages now handles the partial bytes at the ends page by page,
then whole shadow bytes eight at a time (64 pages, 256 KiB of guest
space, per step), and reads before it writes, so shadow pages that were
never written stay unpopulated. The bits it clears and the invalidation
it requests are the same as before: InvalidateGuestRange only when a
page in the range was executable. When start + size overflows, the
range is rounded exactly as before.

New translator stats: clear_executable_calls, clear_executable_pages and
clear_executable_ns, so a profile shows the calls and their sizes.

Tests: guest_map_shadow_test.cc now also runs in the arm64 suite (it
only ran in the riscv64 one), with a 4000-operation differential test
against a page-by-page model across byte and word boundaries, a 64 GiB
never-executable range (neighbours kept, the shadow not dirtied), a
wrapped range that rounds to nothing, and the counters with and without
an invalidation.
